  <bp:ProteinReference rdf:ID="ProteinReference6">
    <bp:organism rdf:resource="#BioSource1" />
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">UniProt:P15428 HPGD</bp:name>
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">HPGD</bp:name>
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">PGDH1</bp:name>
    <bp:name rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">SDR36C1</bp:name>
    <bp:xref rdf:resource="#UnificationXref133" />
    <bp:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION Catalyzes the NAD-dependent dehydrogenation (oxidation) of a broad array of hydroxylated polyunsaturated fatty acids (mainly eicosanoids and docosanoids, including prostaglandins, lipoxins and resolvins), yielding their corresponding keto (oxo) metabolites (PubMed:10837478, PubMed:16757471, PubMed:16828555, PubMed:21916491, PubMed:25586183, PubMed:8086429). Decreases the levels of the pro-proliferative prostaglandins such as prostaglandin E2 (whose activity is increased in cancer because of an increase in the expression of cyclooxygenase 2) and generates oxo-fatty acid products that can profoundly influence cell function by abrogating pro-inflammatory cytokine expression (PubMed:15574495, PubMed:25586183). Converts resolvins E1, D1 and D2 to their oxo products, which represents a mode of resolvin inactivation. Resolvin E1 plays important roles during the resolution phase of acute inflammation, while resolvins D1 and D2 have a unique role in obesity-induced adipose inflammation (PubMed:16757471, PubMed:22844113).CATALYTIC ACTIVITY prostaglandin E2 + NAD(+) = 15-oxoprostaglandin E2 + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY (15S)-hydroxy-(5Z,8Z,11Z,13E)-eicosatetraenoate + NAD(+) = 15-oxo-(5Z,8Z,11Z,13E)-eicosatetraenoate + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY (11R)-hydroxy-(5Z,8Z,12E,14Z)-eicosatetraenoate + NAD(+) = 11-oxo-(5Z,8Z,12E,14Z)-eicosatetraenoate + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY lipoxin A4 + NAD(+) = 15-oxo-(5S,6R)-dihydroxy-(7E,9E,11Z,13E)-eicosatetraenoate + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY 15-oxo-(5S,6R)-dihydroxy-(7E,9E,11Z)-eicosatrienoate + NADH + H(+) = (5S,6R,15S)-trihydroxy-(7E,9E,11Z)-eicosatrienoate + NAD(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY prostaglandin A1 + NAD(+) = 15-oxo-prostaglandin A1 + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY prostaglandin E1 + NAD(+) = 15-oxoprostaglandin E1 + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY 14-hydroxy-(4Z,7Z,10Z,12E,16Z,19Z)-docosahexaenoate + NAD(+) = 14-oxo-(4Z,7Z,10Z,12E,16Z,19Z)-docosahexaenoate + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY resolvin E1 + NAD(+) = 18-oxo-resolvin E1 + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY resolvin D1 + NAD(+) = 8-oxoresolvin D1 + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY resolvin D1 + NAD(+) = 17-oxoresolvin D1 + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY resolvin D2 + NAD(+) = 7-oxoresolvin D2 + NADH + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY resolvin D2 + NAD(+) = 16-oxoresolvin D2 + NADH + H(+)BIOPHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES kcat is 8.6 min(-1) with (11R)-hydroxy-(5Z,8Z,12E,14Z)-eicosatetraenoate and (5Z,8Z,11Z,13E,15S)-hydroxyeicosatetraenoate as substrates.SUBUNIT Homodimer.SUBCELLULAR LOCATION Detected in colon epithelium (at protein level).INDUCTION Down-regulated by cortisol, dexamethasone and betamethasone. Down-regulated in colon cancer. Up-regulated by TGFB1.DISEASE The disease is caused by variants affecting the gene represented in this entry.DISEASE The disease is caused by variants affecting the gene represented in this entry.DISEASE The disease is caused by variants affecting the gene represented in this entry.SIMILARITY Belongs to the short-chain dehydrogenases/reductases (SDR) family.</bp:comment>
  </bp:ProteinReference>
